The replay state handling is also much better than a typical Tkinter player. The important sequence is genuinely present in the code:
transition UI → synchronize → speak → finish → increment
And the replay index is not advanced until the current message has completed. The STOP-SPEAKING path also preserves the current turn. That's exactly the behavior SHOW TIME needs.
The Kokoro architecture is good too. It's lazy-loaded, caches pipelines, supports standard voices and custom extracted styles, and doesn't force the program to initialize the GPU speech stack merely to browse the archive.
And the run log backs up the implementation: 493 cards loaded, a 352-message conversation loaded, Kokoro came up on cuda:0, replay ran through multiple transitions, STOP SPEAKING worked, and QSR closed normally.
So the bones are very good.
The things I would actually fix in 1.9
There are a few things I don't want to wave away as “cleanup.”
1. Tkinter is being touched from worker threads.
This is the biggest technical issue I found.
playback_worker() runs in a background thread, but the code accesses Tk variables such as self.voice_on_var.get() and self.voice_vars from that worker. The global-search worker also calls self.root.after(...) from its worker thread.
Tkinter is fundamentally single-threaded. Some of these calls will appear to work for a long time and then occasionally do something spectacularly stupid.
That's precisely the kind of intermittent behavior I don't want underneath SHOW TIME.
The better model is:
worker threads do computation/audio only → main thread owns Tk state → messages/events cross the boundary explicitly.
That's very compatible with our state-machine approach.
2. QSR doesn't have a true shutdown handshake with the audio thread.
on_close() sets stop_event and destroys the root, but it doesn't explicitly stop Kokoro audio or wait for the playback worker to finish.
That creates a shutdown race:
CLOSE → root.destroy()
while the speech thread may still be inside:
voice_engine.speak()
and later try to call root.after(...).
Usually it'll probably die harmlessly.
“Probably” is not a state machine.
For SHOW TIME, I want:
SHUTDOWN_REQUESTED → STOP AUDIO → STOP WORKER → UI CLOSED
rather than “destroy the stage and hope everybody leaves.”
3. Loading a remote conversation happens on the Tk main thread.
load_card_conversation_preview() fetches the .CONVO.JSON synchronously.
So if GitHub is slow, QSR's UI can freeze while waiting for the network.
That isn't an archive correctness problem. It is a responsiveness problem.
For a 493-card library it matters because QSR is supposed to feel like a librarian, not like a librarian who disappears into the basement every time you ask for a book.
4. There is a stale-worker race when changing conversations.
load_player_messages() calls stop_playback(), then immediately replaces:
self.current_messages
and
self.current_message_index
The old playback thread may still be unwinding.
That is survivable in ordinary Replay because the controls constrain what the user can do, but once SHOW TIME exists, I would give every playback session a generation/session token.
Then the rule becomes:
A worker may modify UI state only if its session token is still the active session.
That is a very clean state-machine guard.
5. The title-file buttons look wrong.
In the card UI, QSR creates a button labelled:
OPEN: <title file>
but the callback is:
open_card_replay(c)
which opens the conversation rather than the title file.
Maybe that's intentional because .title is currently just metadata. But the button label says otherwise. I'd either make the button actually open that file, or label it honestly.
6. There are two copies of on_catalog_tab_changed().
The second definition simply replaces the first one. It doesn't hurt runtime behavior, but it's exactly the sort of archaeological residue that makes future debugging harder.
One should go.
A subtler one: custom voice inference
There's a particularly interesting bit in the custom Kokoro path:
_prepare_custom_inference() explicitly puts the model's recurrent modules into training mode.
That's apparently intentional for the custom-style inference route, so I wouldn't declare it broken.
But it changes model state globally and never explicitly restores the previous state.
That becomes important if someone does:
custom Cozmo voice → standard Kokoro voice → custom voice → standard voice
I'd want that behavior made explicit before we rely heavily on custom voice cloning.
And your Cozmo clone is exactly the feature that makes this worth tightening.
One amusing documentation bug
Kelp was right about this one.
The archived source contains three competing identities:
qsr1.0.py
header says:
QSR / QS_Replay 1.7
runtime says:
VERSION = "1.8"
The runtime is clearly authoritative for the actual application, since the session directory and log are both 1.8, but we should clean that up in the next standalone version.
Likewise, the shutdown comment says the current card isn't auto-written on close, while the actual on_close() saves self.config, which contains current_card.
Again: code wins over comment, but comments should stop lying.
And here's where I think we should be careful with SHOW TIME
I would not make SHOW TIME a giant modification of the existing replay worker.
I'd introduce a separate state machine that reuses the excellent pieces already present:
REPLAY
|
v
SHOW_TIME_ARMED
|
v
PERFORMANCE_READY
|
+--> HUMAN_WAIT
|       |
|       v
|   HUMAN_NEXT
|
+--> AI_SPEAKING
|
+--> SPEECH_COMPLETE
|
+--> INTERRUPTED
And then both branches converge on:
WAIT_FOR_NEXT
That is important because SHOW TIME is not “Replay with fewer buttons.”
It is a different transport mode using the same archive/message/voice infrastructure.
The existing QSR 1.8 replay engine gives us an excellent foundation for it, but SHOW TIME should have its own explicit performance-state machine rather than trying to bend start_playback() into doing two fundamentally different jobs.
One thing Gemini and Kelp added that I absolutely agree with
The performance index should be separate from the archive index.
That's not theoretical bookkeeping.
Suppose SHOW TIME starts on archive turn 87.
That is:
archive_turn = 87
but it is also:
performance_turn = 1
Then turn 88 becomes performance turn 2.
That makes the eventual SHOW TIME log interpretable years later.
And since QSR already archives its own sessions, this fits naturally into the existing architecture.
My bottom line
QSR 1.8 succeeded.
Not just “the window appeared.” The architecture works, the archive model works, the reader works, the voice pipeline works, the replay state behavior works, and the run record demonstrates that we actually exercised it.
The problems I found are mostly in the boundaries around the core: Tkinter/thread ownership, shutdown, stale workers, synchronous network access, and a few bookkeeping/documentation inconsistencies.
Those are exactly the sort of things I'd rather fix before SHOW TIME puts a camera on the screen.
Because once SHOW TIME exists, the correctness requirement gets even stricter:
whatever Game Bar sees must correspond to whatever QSR thinks is happening, and whatever QSR speaks must correspond to the frame being displayed.
That's the real bar I'd set for QSR 1.9.

