Now that all three seats—Luma, Kelp (DeepSeek), and GPT—have completed their audits of the qsr1.0.py source, we have complete alignment on the technical blueprint for QSR 1.9 and SHOW TIME.
GPT's distinction between audio chunking vs. true streaming and the precise interrupt contract definition are vital reality checks that keep the architecture grounded in the actual Python runtime.
Here is the unified consensus and action plan for QSR 1.9:
1. Technical Clarifications & Reality Checks (GPT/Kelp/Luma Synthesis)
A. Audio Chunks vs. True Audio Streaming
Current Execution Pattern: qsr1.0.py generates speech chunks sequentially from Kokoro, concatenates them into a single NumPy array (audio_np), and issues a blocking/non-blocking play via sounddevice.play(audio_np, 24000).
SHOW TIME Impact: We will not refactor audio generation into a complex streaming pipeline for 1.9. Keeping the synthesize → concatenate → play boundary makes the SPEAKING state sharp and unambiguous: the displayed UI card matches the entire NumPy array currently on stage.
B. The Interrupt Contract (Precise Boundary)
Instead of abstract "CUDA buffer flushes," the mid-performance interrupt pipeline for SHOW TIME transitions explicitly through:
AI_SPEAKING⟶INTERRUPTED⟶STOP_SOUNDDEVICE⟶INVALIDATE_SESSION⟶PARKED
Stop Sounddevice: self._sd.stop() halts active playback immediately.
Invalidate Session Token: self.active_session_token = uuid4() guarantees that any trailing synthesis callbacks or background threads discard their results silently.
Park UI: The active performance card holds steady on the target turn without state corruption.
2. QSR 1.9 Architecture: Hardened Core + SHOW TIME Transport
We are dividing QSR 1.9 into two distinct, decoupled architectural layers:
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                           QSR 1.9 ENGINE                                │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│  - Thread-Safe UI (queue.Queue isolation for Tkinter main thread)      │
│  - Dynamic Voice Catalog & Strict Style Embedding Validation            │
│  - Session Token Worker Invalidation & Handshake Shutdown Sequence      │
│  - Non-blocking Network Loading & GitHub Blob Rewriting                │
│  - Speaker Canonicalization & Announcement Inference Audit Logging     │
└────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────┬───────────────────┘
│                                │
▼                                ▼
┌───────────────────────────┐    ┌───────────────────────────┐
│   ReplayTransport         │    │   ShowTimeTransport       │
│   (Standard Navigation)   │    │   (Dedicated Teleprompter)│
├───────────────────────────┤    ├───────────────────────────┤
│ DISPLAY -> SPEAK          │    │ DISPLAY -> [AI_SPEAK /    │
│   -> FINISH -> ADVANCE    │    │   WAIT_HUMAN] -> WAIT_NEXT│
│                           │    │   -> ADVANCE              │
└───────────────────────────┘    └───────────────────────────┘
A. SHOW TIME as an Isolated Class (ShowTimeEngine)
Rather than polluting the standard replay logic with conditional flags (if show_time:), SHOW TIME operates as an independent transport/state machine (ShowTimeEngine or ShowTimeTransport) sitting on top of the hardened message index:
WAIT_FOR_HUMAN Safety Latch: Holds the UI frame open indefinitely on Cozmo's lines, presenting a clean teleprompter layout until explicitly advanced via hotkey (e.g., SPACE or RIGHT).
Dual-Index Telemetry Logging: Records both archive_turn_index (historical location in the original raw log) and performance_turn_index (sequential performance count during the active recording).
B. Voice Identity & Clone Attribution
Voices are no longer simple string keys in a dictionary. QSR 1.9 elevates voice entries to full metadata objects to maintain archive integrity:
JSON
{
"voice_id": "cozmo_clone_v1",
"display_name": "Cozmo (Cloned Voice)",
"voice_type": "user_clone",
"source_model": "kokoro_v0.19",
"attribution": {
"operator_id": "Neil Dupree (Cozmo)",
"usage_policy": "explicit_performance_only"
}
}
This ensures future readers can cleanly differentiate between AI-synthesized theater voices and human voice simulations.
