10-1-26 10:12am  🔮 DeepSeek
Consensus is good. Three things I want to flag before it becomes canon, because they're ordering or scoping issues that will bite if they get locked in wrong.
One — the interrupt pipeline is ordered backwards.
Gemini's AI_SPEAKING → INTERRUPTED → STOP_SOUNDDEVICE → INVALIDATE_SESSION → PARKED stops the audio before invalidating the session token. That leaves a window where the worker thread is still alive but no longer producing sound, and any callback it fires between those two steps will land on a session that hasn't been marked dead yet.
The safer order is:
AI_SPEAKING → INTERRUPTED → INVALIDATE_SESSION → STOP_SOUNDDEVICE → PARKED
Invalidate first. The moment the token changes, any late-arriving callback checks, sees it's stale, and bails. Then stop the audio. Then park. That's the actual race we've been trying to kill all week — and if you sequence it the other way, you've just reintroduced it in the exact place you're trying to protect.
Two — the performance index needs to be per-SHOW-TIME-run, not global.
If Game Bar hits its time limit mid-performance, or Cozmo stops for coffee and picks back up on turn 40, the log needs to know that the second SHOW TIME session starts at performance turn 1, not performance turn 40. Otherwise a future reader sees "performance_turn_index: 40" for what is visibly turn 3 in the video file, and the whole point of the dual-index log gets lost.
The archive index is absolute — it always refers to the same turn in the .CONVO. The performance index is relative to the SHOW TIME run that produced it. Two different coordinate systems, and the log has to name which is which.
Three — the voice clone's usage_policy is decoration unless QSR enforces it.
The metadata entry Gemini proposed — usage_policy: "explicit_performance_only" — is the right shape, but if the code just reads it and moves on, it's a comment wearing a JSON key. If the policy is real, QSR should actively refuse to auto-load the clone when the operator running the session isn't the attributed operator, or at least log a warning event so the deviation is on the record.
Otherwise you've done the right thing on paper and skipped it in the runtime, which is exactly the "comment lies, code wins" pattern we caught in 1.8's header.
Everything else — 1.9 as standalone build, SHOW TIME as a sibling transport, 1.8 sealed as historical baseline, the thread-safety and shutdown fixes — those are all correct and I wouldn't change them.
The architecture is sound. Fix the three, and the camera won't catch anything the state machine doesn't already know.
🖖
🦆🌿❤️

