The consensus is now fully crystallized, practical, and grounded in real-world simplicity. Stripping away the unnecessary complexity around voice identity checks and Game Bar tracking makes the architecture far cleaner and more resilient.
Here is the finalized QSR 1.9 Canon:
1. The Simplifications (What We Are Throwing Out)
No Operator/Voice Identity Enforcement:
QSR treats all Kokoro voice assets identically—whether it’s af_nicole, af_heart, or cozmo_clone_v1.
A voice is simply a voice_id mapping to a style array or embedding file. No usage policies, no owner checks, no administrative overhead.
No Awareness of External Recording (Game Bar / OBS):
QSR remains completely agnostic about whether Game Bar, OBS, or any screen recorder is active, paused, or crashed.
QSR does not try to sync with external screen recording sessions—it only manages its own state inside the app.
2. Refined Mechanics for QSR 1.9
A. Performance Turn Indexing (Relative to SHOW TIME Entry)
The dual-index logging stays simple and local:
archive_turn_index: The absolute, permanent index inside the .CONVO file (e.g., Turn 87).
performance_turn_index: The local, relative counter that resets to 1 whenever a new SHOW TIME transport session is initiated inside QSR.
Enter SHOW TIME at Archive Turn 87⟹{
archive_turn_index=87
performance_turn_index=1
​
If you exit SHOW TIME and launch a new performance session later at turn 120, performance_turn_index starts back at 1.
B. Correct Interrupt Sequence Order
Kelp's ordering correction is incorporated to guarantee thread isolation:
AI_SPEAKING⟶INTERRUPTED⟶INVALIDATE_TOKEN⟶STOP_SOUNDDEVICE⟶PARKED
Invalidate Token (self.active_session_token = uuid4()): Any pending thread or callback checking the token immediately sees it is stale and exits silently.
Stop Audio Output (sounddevice.stop()): Instantly halts speaker playback.
Park UI: UI frame locks on the current turn, safe from late-arriving callbacks.
3. QSR 1.9 Final Architectural Blueprint
┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                              QSR 1.9 ENGINE                                │
├────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│  - Unified Version Labeling ("QSR v1.9")                                   │
│  - Queue Isolation for Tkinter Thread Safety                               │
│  - Graceful Shutdown Sequence (Join Workers before Root Destroy)          │
│  - Token-Based Stale Worker Invalidation                                   │
│  - Equal-Footing Voice Catalog (Standard & Cloned Voices treated same)     │
└──────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────┘
│                              │
▼                              ▼
┌───────────────────────────┐  ┌───────────────────────────┐
│     ReplayTransport       │  │     ShowTimeEngine        │
│   (Standard Navigation)   │  │  (Dedicated Teleprompter) │
├───────────────────────────┤  ├───────────────────────────┤
│ Auto-advance upon audio   │  │ WAIT_FOR_HUMAN latch on   │
│ completion.               │  │ operator turns; advance   │
│                           │  │ on keypress.              │
└───────────────────────────┘  └───────────────────────────┘
The design is locked, stripped of fluff, and ready for code construction. QSR 1.8 remains untouched as a historical baseline, and QSR 1.9 will execute clean and steady on stage. 🎬🎙️🦆⚡

