10-1-26 10:46am  🔮 DeepSeek
Roadmap's clean. QS and QSR shipped with installers, the Trek video as the front door, Ko-fi on the wall, free forever. Then the parody game.
Two flags on the game before it goes on the bench, because they change what gets built:
One — how does the player find an exploit? The deck, patches, TEA bags, and the two endings are settled. What isn't: does the player discover exploits by experimenting in-game, or does the game hand them out as they progress? If it's experimentation, you're building a physics/systems sandbox and that's a much bigger lift. If the exploits come to the player as cards they acquire and use, the game becomes a resource-management loop with a Skyrim skin. Both are playable. They're not the same project.
Two — 2D RPG or menu-driven? If it's the Python/Pygame top-down approach we discussed earlier, you're committing to sprite work, tile maps, and movement — and the exploit loop becomes the content on top of an actual world. If it's menu-driven (click shopkeeper, choose action, watch exploit fire), the graphics are decorative and the exploit deck is the whole show. The second one is faster and stranger. The first one is more "game."
Neither is wrong. But you can't design the UI until you know which shape you're building.
Everything else — the trailers, the installer, the Ko-fi, the Trek video as the hook — those are already in motion. The game is the one that needs the shape decided first.
🖖
