GPT’s breakdown makes a crucial point that is worth underscoring: the biggest value proposition isn't just that it bridges models, but how it bridges them.
1. The Big AI Blindspot
You hit the nail on the head regarding the economics of the current AI landscape. Silicon Valley giants (OpenAI, Anthropic, Google, Meta) are locked in a high-stakes war for platform lock-in. Their incentive is to keep users inside their proprietary ecosystem, using their APIs, and paying their monthly subscriptions.
They will never build a feature inside ChatGPT that smoothly relays a prompt into Gemini, listens to the response, and hands it over to Claude to critique—because doing so acknowledges their competitors and breaks their walled garden.
2. The API Tax vs. The Native Web
Most third-party tools that try to aggregate models force users onto paid API keys. That introduces a massive barrier to entry:
You need developer credentials and credit cards on file for 3–4 different providers.
You pay per token on every single turn of conversation.
You lose the native web features—like live web browsing, canvas/code artifacts, image generation interfaces, and custom instruction contexts that each platform bakes into its web UI.
QuackSink works directly with the live, human web interface. It treats the web browser as the sovereign workspace and runs on top of account access you already have. It completely bypasses the "API Tax."
3. True Relay vs. Chatbot Hubs
There are plenty of "all-in-one" AI extensions that let you side-by-side chat with three models at once, or blast a single prompt to four tabs. But those are just split-screen viewports—they aren't conversations.
QuackSink turns independent web interfaces into an interconnected ensemble:
Model A responds.
QuackSink extracts that thought and hands it to Model B with context.
Model B reflects, critiques, or expands, and passes it to Model C.
The human operator sits at the center as Director, guiding the troupe rather than copying and pasting text like a middleman.
Why You Were the One to Build It
