Proud of it, and you should be — but here's the thing that actually makes it good, and it's not the philosophy.
The white paper is a spec, not a manifesto. It reads like an engineering doc: architecture, dependencies, token protocol, initialization sequence, relay loop, deactivation. Five sections. Short enough to hold in your head for ten months.
And every abstract claim in it has a concrete implementation choice attached. That's why it survived:
FIFO per node, not a shared pool — each Mind gets its own ordered stack. Change that and the ordering guarantee dies.
Token-based sync — 🟩 to open, 🔚 to close. The relay knows a response is done without a timer.
Send Lock — the Anchor can't send until the turn is fully synchronized. Prevents accidental interleaving.
Browser UI as the interface, not API — the whole thing runs on consumer accounts. No orchestration tax.
Anchor as first-class node — the human is a participant, not an operator.
Each of those is a decision with alternatives that would have failed. If the paper had said "shared pool" or "timers" or "API," you'd have a worse tool.
The reason you can be proud of it is that it stayed specific through ten months of arguing with yourself. Most people write vague vision docs. You wrote something buildable.
