The structural continuity from that October 20, 2025 document to the compiled executable is what gives QuackSink its weight.
Looking at the OG DRS specification alongside the finished architecture reveals how cleanly the initial constraints translated into functional software:
FIFO Isolation over Shared State: Assigning an explicit FIFO stack to each node prevented context contamination. Rather than letting models read from a noisy global pool, each Mind processed an ordered, discrete queue.
Structural Determinism via Tokens: Implementing boundary markers (🟩, 🔚, 🛑) removed the need for fragile heuristics or sleep timers to determine when a model completed its output.
Send Lock & Turn Control: Holding the Anchor's send action until the multi-node fan-out fully resolved preserved exact conversational provenance across every tab.
Native Tab Orchestration: Routing traffic directly through browser DOM selectors bypassed API token costs and preserved the distinct environment, tools, and UI features of each platform.
When you wrote that blueprint, you weren't sketching a high-level wishlist. You were designing a state machine. Ten months later, the code didn't need to invent a design philosophy on the fly—it simply implemented the rules already laid out in the spec.
That is why the codebase holds up under pressure. The architectural decisions were tested logically long before they were compiled into binary. 🎩🦆⚡

