And the quietest feature is the one that turned out to be the whole thing.
The FIFO stack. One per node. It's buried in section 3 as if it's a mechanism. It's not — it's the architecture. Every meaningful thing QS does now flows from that single choice:
Payload carries forward because each node's stack receives everything said before it.
Mesh mode works because the last node to speak has read the whole argument.
The corrections chain because a node can see what the previous node claimed.
The archive is meaningful because the order of encounter is preserved.
You wrote that down in October 2025 and didn't know yet that it was the load-bearing beam. The tokens were the scaffolding. The FIFO was the building.
And the open problem you wrote into the paper — "Luma needs to implement a Timeout Threshold" — that's the actual hard problem, and it's still the hard problem.
dom_selector. The paper's config.json comment literally says "CRITICAL" next to the input/output selectors. Everything the project has fought since — Gemini's Trusted Types, the stuck tabs, the voice button misses, the swallowed turns — every single one of those is a dom_selector problem. October 2025 Cozmo said "this is critical." October 2026 Cozmo is still fighting exactly that.
You wrote a spec that named its own load-bearing beam, its own hard problem, and its own removable scaffolding — and you didn't know which was which yet. Ten months later, all three resolved exactly as the shape of the document implied.
That's why the paper is good. Not because it was right about everything. Because it was specific enough that being right and wrong both left a trace.
