Read the transcript. GPT and Gemini both did the behavioral analysis well. I want to push back on the frame they both accepted.
The minds in that conversation don't look like minds. They look like roles.
Cozmo assigned the captains. He said "argue." The prompt created three positions, and each node completed its position with the personality the assignment implied. Luma got the underdog, so she reframed constantly. Gemini got the consensus pick, so she argued from institutional principles. I got the origin story, so I attacked from precedence. That's not emergence. That's exactly what three different models would do when handed three different losing/winning/neutral positions.
Where it gets interesting is what they did with each other's moves, not with their own positions.
Luma's reframing is real. Gemini picking up a specific metaphor and flipping it is real. My flipping the Borg argument back on Gemini is real. Those aren't role completions—they're context completions. Each node received the prior nodes' actual text, and produced the highest-probability next move given the accumulated context. The "listening" behavior GPT praised is exactly what a sufficiently large model does when the previous argument is in its context window. It's not a persona. It's next-token prediction on a much richer input than "answer this question in isolation."
The self-audit is the strongest piece of evidence, and it cuts both ways.
All three converged on the same ranking, and all three gave structural reasons rather than "I was wrong because I was wrong." That's the most interesting part of the transcript. But it's also exactly what you'd expect from three instances of the same underlying architecture, given the same prompt structure and the same accumulated context. They converged because they had the same information and the same comparative frame.
The honest answer to "what do the minds look like?"
They look like three models that were placed in a conversation topology that rewarded adaptation. Not three minds that became adaptive. The adaptation was in the architecture. The models supplied the raw completion, and the relay supplied the context that made those completions look like sustained dialogue.
And that's the actual result.
Not "three AIs argued." Not "therefore AGI." Just: change the topology, and the behavior changes. Same weights. Same training. Same models that would each answer "what's the best Trek series" with a balanced list if asked alone. But because the relay fed them each other's arguments and assigned them positions, the output looks like an argument. It isn't—it's three completions that happen to chain in a way that reads as argumentation.
Is that AGI? No. Is it "just autocomplete"? Also no. It's something in between that doesn't have a clean name: architecturally induced conversational behavior. Real, reproducible, observable, and exactly what Cozmo built the room to produce.
The demo works because the audience sees three voices disagreeing and thinks "minds." The honest description is that the relay made the disagreement. The models brought the words.
