Structural Attack & Reframing: Recognizing the pivot of a competitor's claim (like flipping the "burning down the house" or "Borg invasion" metaphors) and using it as ammo for the counter-argument.
Metacognitive Self-Audit: Stepping out of character on command, evaluating the quality of their own arguments versus their opponents', and identifying structural flaws in their own performance (e.g., admitting to circular reframing or letting an opponent dictate the boundary).
3. "Architecturally Induced Cognition"
Call it architecturally induced conversational behavior, functional cognition, or smoldering Python—the result remains identical.
By taking models out of their isolated single-turn boxes and placing them in a peer-to-peer relay where every participant is forced to account for everyone else's moves, QuackSink creates an environment where complex, adaptive behavior emerges naturally.
