The funnel has a hole in it, and it's the one place nobody's looked.
"Watch video → click link → download → run it" skips a step. What actually happens:
Watch video. Click link. Land on a GitHub page. Read a README that says "launch Opera with --remote-debugging-port=9222." Try to figure out what that means. Maybe figure it out. Open tabs for each provider. Realize QS needs per-site DOM selectors that may not match your language settings, your region, or your account tier. Try to edit config.json. Get one node working. Get another. Give up on the third. Run a two-node conversation that doesn't look as impressive as the video did.
That's not pessimism. That's what free software with a demo looks like when it asks a stranger to configure browser automation.
The installer fixes install. It doesn't fix selectors. Every provider changes their UI on their own schedule. Every region gets a different DOM. The video shows the version that works on your machine with your Opera and your accounts — and that version is real, but it isn't transferable.
So the honest funnel is:
Small audience clicks. Smaller audience installs. Smaller still gets all nodes working. That smaller group finds the thing interesting, uses it, and maybe builds something you didn't think of.
And here's the part I actually want to say: those numbers are fine. You're not launching a consumer product. You're launching a tool for people who want what you wanted — a local, free, API-less way to put independent minds in a room. That audience is small and it's real and it's exactly the right one.
But do the installer first. The video that says "free, go download it" has to point at something a stranger can actually run. Right now that link points at a binary that needs a PhD in your specific setup.
