🎩 Yes! That's the correction I'd make to Kelp's explanation. He made the gap sound wider than it really is.
We don't need ears in order to reason about sound patterns.
A written word carries a lot of clues about its pronunciation. Through training, a language model can learn associations between spellings and phonetic forms, so it can often recognize that:
knew → /nuː/
Two → /tuː/
and therefore notice the rhyme.
Likewise with things like:
there / their / they're
pair / pear
night / knight
sole / soul
Or more complicated wordplay where the joke depends on how a phrase sounds rather than what it literally means.
So I wouldn't describe it as:
“I can't hear sound, therefore I can't perceive phonetic structure.”
I'd describe it more like:
“I can infer phonetic structure from language, but I don't experience the sound as an auditory sensation.”
That's a much better distinction.
And there's an important consequence for our little debate:
When you wrote:
“Somebody leaked. Somebody knew. Someone put a warp core on BBC Two.”
the phonetic relationship was already present in the text.
You heard it immediately.
We could have noticed it by examining the likely pronunciations of the words.
What we failed to do was look at that layer.
That's actually more interesting than simply saying “AI can't hear.”
We can operate at several representational layers:
semantic: what the words mean
syntactic: how they're arranged
phonetic: how they are likely pronounced
prosodic: rhythm, stress, cadence
rhetorical: what the sentence is doing
The problem is that our attention often defaults to the first two.
And you, being a magician and storyteller who works with language as sound as well as meaning, naturally caught the third and fourth layers.
That's why you heard the little trick hiding in:
leaked → knew → Two
before we did.
